        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.144,
            0.095,
            0.852,
            0.115
          ],
          "content": "**8. Why does dry HCL gas not show acidic behaviour in the absence of water?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.144,
            0.117,
            0.89,
            0.154
          ],
          "content": "**9. Five solutions A,B,C,D and E when tested with universal indicator showed pH as 4,1,11,7 and 9, respectively. Which solution is**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.153,
            0.294,
            0.17
          ],
          "content": "**(a) neutral?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.172,
            0.17,
            0.376,
            0.188
          ],
          "content": "**(b) strongly alkaline?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.188,
            0.358,
            0.206
          ],
          "content": "**(c) strongly acidic?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.173,
            0.206,
            0.349,
            0.223
          ],
          "content": "**(d) weakly acidic?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.222,
            0.366,
            0.24
          ],
          "content": "**(e) weakly alkaline?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.239,
            0.764,
            0.258
          ],
          "content": "**Arrange the pH in increasing order of hydrogen-ion concentration.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.261,
            0.891,
            0.332
          ],
          "content": "**10. Equal lengths of magnesium ribbons are taken in test tubes A and B. Hydrochloric** acid (HCl) is added to test tube A, while acetic acid (CH COOH) is added to test **tube B. Amount and concentration taken for both the acids are same. In which test tube will the fizzing occur more vigorously and why?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.334,
            0.889,
            0.37
          ],
          "content": "**11. Fresh milk has a pH of 6. How do you think the pH will change as it turns into curd? Explain your answer.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.373,
            0.766,
            0.391
          ],
          "content": "**12. A milkman adds a very small amount of baking soda to fresh milk.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.172,
            0.391,
            0.821,
            0.409
          ],
          "content": "**(a) Why does he shift the pH of the fresh milk from 6 to slightly alkaline?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.172,
            0.408,
            0.666,
            0.427
          ],
          "content": "**(b) Why does this milk take a long time to set as curd?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.429,
            0.858,
            0.448
          ],
          "content": "**13. Plaster of Paris should be stored in a moisture-proof container. Explain why?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.451,
            0.652,
            0.47
          ],
          "content": "**14. What is a neutralisation reaction? Give two examples.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.472,
            0.703,
            0.491
          ],
          "content": "**15. Give two important uses of washing soda and baking soda.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.17,
            0.534,
            0.456,
            0.563
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Group Activity"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.59,
            0.421,
            0.608
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "(I) Prepare your own indicator"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.611,
            0.424,
            0.628
          ],
          "content": "Crush beetroot in a mortar."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.633,
            0.546,
            0.65
          ],
          "content": "n **Add sufficient water to obtain the extract.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.654,
            0.759,
            0.672
          ],
          "content": "Filter the extract by the procedure learnt by you in earlier classes."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.675,
            0.783,
            0.693
          ],
          "content": "Collect the filtrate to test the substances you may have tasted earlier."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.696,
            0.893,
            0.749
          ],
          "content": "Arrange four test tubes in a test tube stand and label them as A,B,C and D. Pour **2 mL each of lemon juice solution, soda-water, vinegar and baking soda solution in them respectively.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.753,
            0.892,
            0.789
          ],
          "content": "Put 2-3 drops of the beetroot extract in each test tube and note the colour change **if any. Write your observation in a Table.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.792,
            0.893,
            0.844
          ],
          "content": "You can prepare indicators by using other natural materials like extracts of red **cabbage leaves, coloured petals of some flowers such as Petunia, Hydrangea and Geranium.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
